text.remarks:
Commences with the meykirti of Rajadhiraja I. It records that for the purpose of conducting special worship by bringing out the *aditya-vara-perumbali* for the deity every Sunday at the Sri Rajaraja-isvaramudaiyar temple in Sivapuram, in Purisai-nadu, a subdivision of Manayin-kottam in Jayangonda Chola-mandalam, paddy measuring thirty-four kalam according to the *Rajakesari* standard was being received as revenue (*kanikkadan*) from the land known as the old *devadana-tupparuv-pattu* granted to this temple. To this, measuring increments (*pari-kuru*) were added at the rate of three-nazhi and uri of paddy per kalam, fixing the total at thirty-five kalam, padakku, and ezhu-nazhi of paddy. Out of this:
(1) Three kalam and three kuruni per year for *kani-amudhu* and *tiruvazhudu*;
(2) For those who perform worship (*tiruaradhani-seivar*), temple treasurers (*tirunnaligai-udaiyar*), six students (*marnigal*), 24 temple dancing women (*patiyar*), *panchasariyar*, lute player (*veena-vasippan*), drum player (*udukkai-vasippan*), singer of *tiruppadiyam*, one possessing the sacred oral tradition (*tiruvay-kezvi-udaiyar*), ten *uvachar* including *padaviyan*, 12 fly-whisk bearers (*kavari-pinakkal*), four temple garland-makers (*tiruppallit-thongaliduvar*), accountant (*kanakkan*), storekeeper (*pandari*), four sacred bodyguards (*tirumey-kappar*), and one who plucks and strings flowers for the sacred bedchamber (*tiruppalli-thamam-parithu-thoduppan*) -- at the rate of padakku of paddy per share, making a total of 29 kalam, thuni, kuruni, and four nazhi for 55 shares per year;
(3) Two kalam of paddy for four lamp-lighters (*vilakku-pidippar*) and four flag-bearers (*kodi-pidippar*).
In total, the record states that thirty-five kalam, padakku, and seven nazhi of paddy were granted.
